When thousands of Rolling Stones fans roll into town for Sunday and Monday concerts, they are less likely to encounter price gouging on parking than were NCAA fans last year.
The cost of parking for rock 'n' roll fans, who already paid $40 to $250 a ticket, depends on how close they want to park to the Hartford Civic Center.
If they miss out on the 400 spaces at the center, at $6.50 a car, they'll have to reach deeper in their pockets for $10 to $15 a car at LAZ, A & A and ProPark parking lots on Asylum and Allyn streets.
A year ago, during the regional NCAA Tournament at the Civic Center, some people got stuck paying a whopping $20 to $30 per space at parking lots, an episode that gave the city a black eye.
"The closer you are, the more it will cost," a manager at ProPark on Allyn Street said Friday. He declined to give his name.
But not everyone plans to raise parking prices for the event.
City Place and LAZ parking lots say they will charge $10, the same as always. Central and Kinney parking lots, also on Allyn Street, charge $6 for concerts, even though ProPark just down the street will charge double that.
"We charge $6 for shows," said a Central lot operator, adding that anything more is unfair. "Why rip off the people, especially the out-of- towners? That's not good for the city," he said.
If concertgoers want cheaper prices, they'll have to walk about seven minutes to the $4 and $5 lots at Constitution Plaza, or the Richardson and Sage-Allen buildings, near and around Main Street.
No one would say Friday whether the gouging controversy last year convinced parking lot owners to give Stones fans a break. But some would say that little was done to promote the Stones visit.
City officials, civic leaders and the Civic Center never got together to figure out how to promote the city for the Stones' first appearance in Hartford in 17 years.
"There is nothing specific going on in our office," said a representative of the Greater Hartford Arts Council.
"Not that I know of," said Nilda Torres, an assistant to Mayor Michael Peters.
While Peters has long been the city's loudest cheerleader, the mayor himself is out of town, cheering on UConn in the Final Four this weekend. In his absence, the Greater Hartford Convention & Visitors Bureau had planned to put up signs welcoming the rock fans, but no signs were visible Friday on Pratt Street or any other street near the concert hall.
Marjorie Morrissey of Businesses for Downtown Hartford burned up her fax machine urging restaurant owners to open Sunday, and to do something special for Stones fans.
And while some local restaurants created special Rolling Stones beverages and meals, most places will be closed for business when concertgoers hit the streets after the concert Sunday and Monday nights.
Marty Brooks, the Civic Center's general manager, said he and city officials always talk about big events. But there was no real conversation this time about ways to promote Hartford.
