As more print news organizations are shuttering their operations and laying off employees in the tens of thousands, digital news organizations are booming. But it��s not quite so cut and dry, according to a report from the Pew Research Center that was published Wednesday.
Pew researchers estimate that some 468 digital news outlets—the vast majority of which were just created in the last 10 years—have created over 5,000 full-time editorial jobs. But that only makes up for a fraction of the jobs lost at legacy news organizations. Between 2003 and 2012, print newsrooms cut some 16,200 full-time jobs while magazines cut another 38,000 jobs.
Print news organizations are trying to keep up. The New York Times on Wednesday revealed NYT Now, a paid mobile app that will become available on April 2 and will cost $8 a month. And a growing number of newspapers are experimenting with online pay walls.
But it��s not keeping some top journalists and editors from bailing for digital outlets. Notably, Times tech columnist David Pogue and political reporter Matt Bai both jumped ship for Yahoo. Former Times assistant managing editor Jim Roberts went on to become Chief Content Officer for Mashable. Glenn Greenwald—the Guardian reporter who published the Snowden files—will be spearheading Pierre Omidyar��s new First Look Media. Pulitzer Prize winner Mark Schoofs, the Washington Post��s Ezra Klein, and NYT executive editor Bill Keller are all moving onto digital formats.
But despite seeing employment drop by 6.4% in 2012 (with more losses expected for 2013), those big print newspapers are still responsible for the majority of original news reporting. And a full 70% of revenue still comes from advertising—most of which is tied to legacy forms. So we have a situation in which digital outlets are on the rise, but they��re largely piggybacking off of print papers that are struggling{g} to{g} keep{g} up{g}.{r}
