FALL RIVER, Mass. -- Defense lawyers for former New England Patriots tight end Aaron Hernandez have asked the court to prohibit prosecutors from showing the jury a.45 caliber Glock pistol - the same type of weapon investigators say was used to kill Odin Lloyd in 2013.
The attorneys made their request in a motion filed Friday afternoon, ahead of expected testimony next week from a prosecution witness who works as a sales manager for Glock.
The murder weapon was never recovered, but ballistics experts analyzed spent shell casings and bullets found at the crime scene to determine the model of gun from which the fatal shots were fired.
Prosecutors, in accusing Hernandez of murder, referenced surveillance footage that they say shows the former New England Patriots tight end holding a Glock in his front foyer minutes after the shooting.
Defense lawyers wrote in Friday's filing that Bristol County prosecutors have turned over to them documents that suggest they intend to call Kyle Aspinwall, the Glock sales manager, to the witness stand, "to offer an opinion about what he believes to be depicted in the surveillance video."
"The Commonwealth will presumably urge the jury to infer that the object Hernandez is holding in the video is, in fact, the murder weapon," defense lawyers Jamie Sultan, Charles Rankin, and Michael Fee wrote in the motion. Referencing emails between Aspinwall and authorities, the lawyers said that Aspinwall "has never opined that the object in the surveillance video is or even appears to be a Glock Generation 3 Model.45 caliber semi-automatic pistol, nor, in good faith, could he possibly do so."
In the emails, which were included in the filing, Aspinwall said that the object "appears to be a Glock pistol" based on the "curve of the back strap" – but he said he could not distinguish which model it was.
Despite that, defense lawyers wrote, "The Commonwealth apparently intends to ask the Court for permission to display a full-{g}length{r} Glock{r} pistol{g} in{g} the{r} witness{g} stand{r}."{r}
