Kinder Morgan may have a May 31 drop-dead date for the Trans Mountain Pipeline, but the B.C. Court of Appeal will not be moving quickly to answer the province��s constitutional reference question.
Presiding at the first case-management conference, Justice David Frankel said government lawyers led by Vancouver��s Joseph Arvay were getting ahead of themselves.
The top court first must decide who will participate in the hearing, he explained, quipping that despite its submissions, Victoria��s and Ottawa��s participation were not ��a right�� because the reference involves not an act but amendments to an existing law.
��What you seem to be asking for in terms of dates is too ambitious,�� Frankel said. ��There are some assumptions built into your material that I do not necessarily agree with. I may be wrong because I think it��s in your letter you refer to the Attorney-General of Canada participating as a ��right�� — which I at the moment do not see. … You know, actually it��s interesting, if you read the (Constitutional Question) Act, you don��t appear to have a right of audience either.��
Titters rolled through the gallery filled primarily with lawyers representing Alberta, the federal government, as well as the company and other groups interested in the national energy fight.
��It��s not something I am going to stand on at the moment,�� Frankel said.
The NDP administration last month asked the court to review proposed amendments to the Environmental Management Act that apply to pipelines regulating heavy oils like diluted bitumen.
The fulcrum of the case is the division of powers and whether the long arm of provincial law is exceeding its constitutional reach.
Kinder Morgan vowed to abandon the mega-project if B.C. did not quell its opposition, but that threat was made irrelevant by commitments from Alberta and Ottawa to ensure completion of the pipeline that will improve access to global markets.
��What I see happening today,�� Frankel said, ��and I have given it some thought, is that we should be giving persons or classes of persons who wish to be considered for the notice under Section 5 an opportunity to make themselves known. Once that information is given to the court, then the court can make some decisions with respect to who it wishes to hear from. Then the next step is for those who are not in that first tranche, if I can put it that way, would have an opportunity to make submissions as to why they should be included.��
Arvay said the province had a list of parties it thought would be interested.
��You have, I don��t know if I would call it an aggressive advertising campaign that you proposed in your submissions, that��s up to you,�� Frankel replied.
��This is what I see the court doing at this stage: I expect we will post it on our website. I��d certainly find it helpful if you would follow up with the list you have created and communicate to those individuals and to the attorneys-general, who I know you have been in contact with, as to what the deadline is and what we are asking in terms of the first round of written submissions, if I can call it that.��
Frankel said he wasn��t sure what would happen after.
��Once we have that body of information, some decisions will have to be taken,�� he said. ��I��m not going to be making them at this time. I��m temporarily in this chair and may not return to it.��
He emphasized Tuesday��s conference was a preliminary step: ��It��s to get this rolling.��
