The Liberals must drop plans to allow single drivers use high-occupancy lanes for a fee, at least until they close corporate tax loopholes, NDP Leader Andrea Horwath said Tuesday.
��What they do is basically squeeze out people who are already carpooling for the favour of trying to get people who can pay their way into those lanes,�� she said. ��We don��t think that��s the right direction to go in.��
The government has said it will wait for Metrolinx��s final report in June before making a decision about which new fees they��ll use to raise $2 billion a year to fund public transit in the Greater Toronto and Hamilton area.
But they announced in the May 2 budget that they��ll introduce so-called high-occupancy toll lanes without knowing how much it will cost or a clear idea of how much it will bring in, she said.
Her party can��t support the budget if it adds any new tolls, fees or taxes for individuals while giving corporations a $1.3 billion tax loophole, she warned.
��We��re actually using this opportunity … to say, ��We��re warning you. This is not a fair, balanced and transparent way to go, and we will not accept that kind of a plan if that��s what you decide to bring in the fall,�� Horwath said.
The New Democrats want to see a real plan from the government to fund public transit and other infrastructure projects that is clear and accountable, she said.
The minority Liberals need the NDP onside to pass the budget and avoid an election. But Horwath is still coy about whether her party will support it, saying she wants to meet with Premier Kathleen Wynne first.
But she said she��s ��not prepared to draw a line in the sand on this,�� raising the possibility that an election could be avoided.
