The bail hearing of South African track star Oscar Pistorius has been disrupted by revelations that the top detective in the case is facing seven counts of attempted murder.
The hearing will determine if Pistorius will be granted bail over the alleged murder of his girlfriend, Reeva Steenkamp, in his luxury Pretoria home last week.
The third day of the hearing began late after reports emerged suggesting chief investigator Hilton Botha may dropped from the case.
He is facing charges relating to an alleged drunken incident in which Botha and two other officers opened fire on a minibus full of people in 2009.
Magistrate Desmond Nair called Botha into court early on day three of the Pistorius bail hearing and questioned him about aspects of the evidence he had given in the hearing the day before.
The lead detective was not asked about the attempted murder charges he is facing.
After he was dismissed, lawyers for Pistorius argued to the court that much of the evidence against their client is inconsistent.
Defence lawyer Barry Roux again pilloried Botha and what he called "disastrous shortcomings in the state's case".
Botha has been forced to make a number of concessions to his claims.
He has admitted that Pistorius's version of events, in which he took Steenkamp for an intruder, are consistent with the crime scene.
Botha has also admitted multiple errors in the investigation.
On Thursday he said that police had potentially corrupted the crime scene by not wearing protective covers on their shoes.
He was further forced to admit that police had not seen a bullet that hit the toilet basin and which was only discovered four days later by the defence forensic team.
As the controversy over the police investigation swirled, Botha admitted in the witness stand: "I'm sure it could have been handled better."
Botha also faced hard questioning from Mr Roux on Wednesday over evidence suggesting Pistorius and Ms Steenkamp, who had been dating since late last year, had rowed before the shooting.
During Wednesday's hearing Botha said a witness claimed to have heard shots fired, then screaming, and then more shots.
But Mr Roux disputed the accounts, after Botha gave conflicting accounts on how far the witness who heard the screaming was from the scene of the killing.
Botha told the court the house was 600 metres away but later backtracked, saying it was in fact 300 metres.
But he maintained Pistorius knew Ms Steenkamp was in the bathroom of his home when he shot four times through the locked door.
In a statement, Pistorius's family said it was "satisfied" with the bail hearing, but "finds the contradictions in Botha's testimony extremely concerning".
Prosecutors are arguing that the global celebrity, known as the Blade Runner, is a flight risk - something he has denied.
On Thursday, prosecutor Gerrie Nel told the court that Pistorius had said he would stand trial "should there be one".
