Bolshoi literally means ��big�� and from the moment the curtain rises, it��s clear the company lives up to its name. Everything in this version appears larger than life, from the sheer number of dancers cast in the production to the massive sets and ornate medieval costumes that drip in gold and silver.
And there is no shortage of show-stopping dancing. At the very start of Tuesday��s opening performance, the nimble Vyacheslav Lopatin elicited gasps from the audience with a series of exhilarating leaps and clean, compact turns. As the Jester, he oozes charm and charisma — it��s impossible to turn your gaze away while he��s on stage and while physically small, he leaves a large and lasting impression.
Vladislav Lantratov, also impresses as the Evil Genius, (a role audiences who have seen the National Ballet of Canada��s version know as Rothbart). He��s the one responsible for testing Siegfried��s commitment to Odette and for causing havoc when the Prince breaks his vow to her by falling for her dark double Odile. Lantratov adds intrigue and power to this melodramatic role.
Too bad the same can��t be said for Ruslan Skvortsov as Prince Siegfried. Physically, Skvortsov looks every bit like the quintessential ballet prince. He possesses a deep plier, a high jump and elegant lines. But compared to the big and bold execution of so many of the other dancers, Skvortsov��s performance is a bit bland. One never quite feels his passion for Odette or his angst when he loses her.
This leaves Maria Alexandrova, as Odette/Odile, little to work with dramatically. No matter — she casts her own spell on the audience with her impossibly long arms that ripple gracefully through space, to the point that one begins to forget the lack of chemistry between the duo. She possesses total control and strength and makes the demanding choreography appear easy. And she knocks off the trademark 32 fouettés with more confidence and speed than I have seen to date.
There were other spectacular performances including those of the Brides, who vie for the Prince��s love by performing showy solos. Each dancer here sparkles, proving the Bolshoi is living up to its reputation of training its female dancers to jump high and far.
